@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@ The Microsoft Agent Framework implementation is available beside the existing cu
 
 The framework runtime uses `AIAgent`, `AgentSession`, Microsoft.Extensions.AI function invocation, the OpenAI-compatible SDK adapter for OpenRouter, and OllamaSharp for Ollama. It does not use `OllamaClient` or `OpenRouterClient`. Classification and SQL repair still use the existing application algorithms through an `ILlmClient` adapter over `IChatClient`.
 
-Both runtimes use the same `ValidatedSqlExecutor`. Model SQL must still pass SQL approval, SqExpress parsing and allow-list validation, read-only checks, HarborFlow security rewriting, and SQL Server execution. Runtime SQL errors are returned to the existing repair loop.
+Both runtimes use the same `ValidatedSqlExecutor`. Final SQL and internal investigation probes must still pass SQL approval, SqExpress parsing and allow-list validation, read-only checks, HarborFlow security rewriting, and SQL Server execution. Runtime SQL errors are returned to the existing repair loop. Investigation policy, limits, and internal-only result disclosure remain application responsibilities rather than Agent Framework features.
 
 ## Real-session comparison
 
@@ -63,6 +63,29 @@ Microsoft Agent Framework is viable here and provides a cleaner standard agent/s
 
 Keep `Custom` as the default for now. In this GLM/OpenRouter sample it was more predictable and cheaper in model calls, while the Framework runtime was stronger at tool-contract adherence. Keeping both behind `AgentRuntime` provides a useful reference implementation and allows future models/framework versions to be compared without changing the database safety layer.
 
+## Investigation mode note
+
+Investigation mode is available as an opt-in tool in both runtimes. It is structurally useful for uncertain literals and zero-row diagnosis, and every probe passes the normal approval, parser, allow-list, read-only, and security-rewriting flow. Probe count, returned rows, selected columns, and visible cells are bounded; internal grids are not printed as final tables.
+
+Interactive CPU-only runs with local `qwen3.8:27b` and the large `SotiConnectManagementServer` schema did not show reliable autonomous probe planning. The model attempted schema browsing, invented columns, ignored tool envelopes in some native/framework calls, and consumed extra repair steps. The validation layer rejected unsafe or broad probes correctly, but could not make the model reason well. Investigation therefore remains disabled by default. It should be enabled only after the chosen model is shown to produce narrow probes consistently; a future typed literal-search tool would be safer and easier for weaker models than unrestricted model-authored probe SQL.
+
+A second round used local `glm-4.7-flash:latest` with reasoning disabled. Custom native tools did not complete and fell back to structured actions. Structured mode found and returned the correct device for a misspelled literal, but needed eight agent steps: it spent two steps on irrelevant probes before executing the useful final query. With the default five steps, the table was returned but the explanation could not complete. Microsoft Agent Framework reached an eight-step limit without a valid answer. For this model and schema, `Custom` plus `ToolCalling: Disabled` was the only tested configuration that completed the investigation flow, and `MaxAgentSteps: 8` was required.
+
+A later prototype retained validated probe values in subsequent system prompts. It was rolled back after a long GLM dialogue: although a narrow `ZD500` probe returned ten candidates, the model ignored them, invented an unrelated device-type uncertainty, repeated investigation, and failed its follow-ups. Retaining raw evidence does not compensate for weak probe planning or evidence selection. Typed literal-search tools remain the recommended next direction.
+
+The same run exposed an independent conversation failure: structured mode could accept an unsolicited `exit` action for a database-overview question. Both runtimes now reject conversation termination unless the user explicitly asks to stop. Database-overview requests also have a deterministic schema-grounded fallback when the model refuses, returns a generic readiness message, or reaches its step limit.
+
+`gemma4:31b` was also tested with reasoning and native tool calling disabled. It produced a strong schema-grounded overview, but failed the core `ZD500` investigation: it spent all three probes exploring join paths instead of the requested literal, then submitted a placeholder value and PostgreSQL-style `LIMIT`. It is suitable for domain summaries but not autonomous investigation on this schema. Both runtime prompts now explicitly prohibit `INFORMATION_SCHEMA`, `sys` catalog views, system tables, metadata functions, and tool calls intended to discover schema.
+
+The application can instead run as an authenticated HTTP MCP server. MCP
+exposes authoritative instructions and the public schema plus deterministic
+`submit_sql` and `investigate_sql` tools. The external agent owns reasoning,
+memory, and SQL correction; this application still owns parsing, schema
+allow-listing, read-only enforcement, investigation limits, result bounding,
+and row-security rewriting. MCP mode does not initialize or call
+Ollama/OpenRouter.
+
+
 ## Local Ollama profiling
 
 A follow-up profile used local Ollama `qwen3.5:4b`, full tools, disabled reasoning, and the same three-message shipment flow for both runtimes. Both produced the same correct results and made exactly 11 model calls.
